                     /note="Domain:cl27024:PlcC Phospholipase C  [Cell
                     wall/membrane/envelope biogenesis]. Members of this protein
                     family are bacterial, phosphatidylcholine-hydrolyzing
                     phospholipase C enzymes, with a characteristic domain
                     architecture as found in hemolytyic (PlcH) and nonhemolytic
                     (PlcN) secreted enzymes of Pseudomonas aeruginosa. PlcH
                     hydrolyzes phosphatidylcholine to diacylglycerol and
                     phosphocholine, but unlike PlcN can also hydrolyze
                     sphingomyelin to ceramide ((N-acylsphingosine)) and
                     phosphocholine. Members of this family share the
                     twin-arginine signal sequence for Sec-independent transport
                     across the plasma membrane. PlcH is secreted as a
                     heterodimer with a small chaperone, PlcR, encoded
                     immediately downstream. [Cellular processes, Pathogenesis]
                     [Superfamily, Aligned:15-560, Eval:4.7e-99, score:307.8]"
                     /note="MGA_3066"
