                     /note="WP_011267514.1 chemotaxis protein CheW (Pseudomonas)
                     [pid:86.8%, q_cov:99.0%, s_cov:99.8%, Eval:2.2e-246]"
                     /note="Domain:cl00256:CheW_like N/A. CheW proteins are part
                     of the chemotaxis signaling mechanism in bacteria. CheW
                     interacts with the methyl accepting chemotaxis proteins
                     (MCPs) and relays signals to CheY, which affects flageller
                     rotation. This family includes CheW and other related
                     proteins that are involved in chemotaxis. The CheW-like
                     regulatory domain in CheA binds to CheW, suggesting that
                     these domains can interact with each other. [Superfamily,
                     Aligned:26-139, Eval:6.3e-13, score:63.9, N-term missing]"
                     /note="Domain:cl00256:CheW_like N/A. CheW proteins are part
                     of the chemotaxis signaling mechanism in bacteria. CheW
                     interacts with the methyl accepting chemotaxis proteins
                     (MCPs) and relays signals to CheY, which affects flageller
                     rotation. This family includes CheW and other related
                     proteins that are involved in chemotaxis. The CheW-like
                     regulatory domain in CheA binds to CheW, suggesting that
                     these domains can interact with each other. [Superfamily,
                     Aligned:345-461, Eval:1.5e-11, score:60.0]"
                     /note="Domain:cl00256:CheW_like N/A. CheW proteins are part
